1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,636
The Word “Begging”

2
00:00:03,636 --> 00:00:34,352
Four thirty in the morning, noon in Tehran. Leila Shirazi finished her analysis of the five conditions and called her aunt. The line went through on the third try. What piece of this war her cables had made, she could not tell her aunt. “Auntie. I’m sorry.” “For what?” “Just because.” A pot lid rattled. “Just because isn’t just because, Leila.” Leila hung up first. If she heard any more she would come apart.

3
00:00:34,952 --> 00:01:05,236
Back at her desk there was a new post up. The president said Iran’s leaders were begging for a deal. The side that is told it is begging fires something to show it is not. She wrote two readings into the cable. Those who want to talk and those who want to fire are split. Warning: insulting public language weakens the side that wants to talk. Someone up the chain might strike that line. She did not. The sentences she did not delete were adding up.

4
00:01:05,836 --> 00:01:37,008
Captain Marcus Webb read the casualty report twice. Prince Sultan. Fifteen. Five critical. The tankers that fueled his aircraft flew from there. He did not know their faces. He knew their voices on the radio. A boom operator with a Texas accent always said the same thing when the refueling was done. Fly safe, Navy. Whether that man was among the fifteen, he had no way to know. His ship was heading to Croatia for repairs.

5
00:01:37,608 --> 00:02:09,068
Two countries started this war, and the blood was running in ten. A base in Saudi Arabia, a worker in Abu Dhabi, the ports of Kuwait. An alliance came down to this: lending your land to someone else’s war, and paying the rent in your own people’s blood. He called the Bahrain number. “This is Webb. Last time I said ‘not yet.’ I’m moving that up. Leave out the name.” The treaties write the obligations, and the blood soaks into a desert runway.

6
00:02:09,668 --> 00:02:13,480
Epic Fury continues in Episode 11.
